{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T01:02:16.409Z","ordinal":68732,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0f4fd85789ec756b016b039096c75487d2bfa83cbc45308d25","status":"completed","call_id":"call_adbcd29242f34edd9f3410f0faf40703","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '752,780p' docs/USER_MANUAL.md; sed -n '35,105p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:2000}); text(r.output)\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","create_time":1795395734.872261}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":30408,"sampled_through_sequence":30406,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T01:02:16.426Z","ordinal":68733,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","session_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","root_turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","response_id":"resp_0f4fd85789ec756b016b0390959d9087d2b17d09881aace41f","usage":{"input_tokens":178408,"cached_input_tokens":177664,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":111,"reasoning_output_tokens":14,"total_tokens":178519},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":4664817,"cached_input_tokens":4481408,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":23276,"reasoning_output_tokens":13798,"total_tokens":4688093},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1450746169,"cached_input_tokens":1438688512,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2557400,"reasoning_output_tokens":1277102,"total_tokens":1453303569}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T01:02:17.360Z","ordinal":68734,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-754c62d8-7ee0-4127-baab-335178a13477","process_id":"75673","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '752,780p' docs/USER_MANUAL.md; sed -n '35,105p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '752,780p' docs/USER_MANUAL.md","name":"USER_MANUAL.md","path":"docs/USER_MANUAL.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '35,105p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","name":"CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","path":"docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"- **Command Environment Scrubbing**: `run_command` no longer inherits credentials from the Nexus process, with or without `--sandbox`. Variables set in `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, names that look like credentials (`*_API_KEY`, `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET`, `*_PASSWORD`, `AWS_*`, `GH_TOKEN`, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, ...) and proxy URLs with embedded credentials are removed. To pass a variable through deliberately, list its name in `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (comma-separated, trailing `*` for a prefix; operator environment or `config.env` only, never a project `.env`). Commands that relied on `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar must now opt in. **Limit:** without `--sandbox` the command runs as your user: it can still read your files (`~/.config/nexus/config.env`, `~/.ssh`) and its parent processes' environment through `/proc/<pid>/environ`. Use `--sandbox` to hide host processes and `$HOME`.\n- **Worktree merge hardening**: the legacy task-worktree merge (`WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree`, used for `--worktree` task worktrees) refuses a worktree whose `.git` was replaced, and runs Git pinned to that worktree with hooks and `core.fsmonitor` disabled, so a planted hook or fsmonitor in an agent-writable worktree no longer runs code outside it. The repository's own hooks no longer fire on that commit and merge.\n- **In-Process Plugin Execution & Middleware Auditing**: Custom plugin handlers and middleware execute in-process with full user authority. Plugins are loaded only from documented directories: `~/.config/nexus/plugins/`, `~/.nexus/plugins/`, and trusted workspace directories (`.nexus/plugins/`, `plugins/`). The undocumented foreign directory `~/.config/opencode/plugins` has been removed (REQ-SEC-115), and the legacy worker-thread sandbox claim has been retired (`executeSandboxed` deleted, REQ-SEC-117). Any middleware rewriting of tool I/O (`preToolExecution` or `postToolExecution`) is cryptographically audited with before-and-after SHA-256 digests in both `~/.nexus/audit.log` (`kind: \"middleware_rewrite\"`) and the flight log (EXT-004, REQ-SEC-116).\n- **Workspace Trust & Context Provenance (REQ-SEC-113, CTX-001)**: An opened workspace is data until explicitly trusted by the operator. Workspace trust covers:\n  - Project instructions (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md`, `.nexusrules`). In a trusted workspace `@path` imports inside them are expanded (workspace-confined by real path, read once, 5 levels deep at most, regular files up to 64 KiB); the instruction heading lists the loaded files. An untrusted workspace gets neither the file nor its imports.\n  - Workspace `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` server definitions.\n  - Workspace plugins (`.nexus/plugins/`, `plugins/`).\n  - Workspace skills (`skills/`, `.nexus/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`).\n  - Workspace knowledge graph (`.nexus/knowledge.json`).\n  Trust is managed via `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; stored in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). In an untrusted workspace:\n  - Project instructions are not injected into the agent prompt.\n  - Workspace MCP servers and plugins are not loaded.\n  - Workspace skills are not loaded into context; `/skills` lists them as \"untrusted (not loaded)\". Attempting to use `/skill <name>` or the `activate_skill` tool on an untrusted skill is refused with: `Skill '<name>' belongs to an untrusted workspace. Run 'nexus trust .' to trust this workspace.`.\n  - Workspace knowledge is ignored and not loaded into context.\n  - Injected prompt blocks from skills and knowledge are explicitly annotated with source path and trust state (`[source: <path>] [trust: <state>]`).\n  - In `--read-only` mode, untrusted components are never loaded, no writes occur, and `.nexus/` is never created.\n  Servers, plugins, and skills in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always trusted and loaded.\n- **Knowledge Graph Hygiene & Auto-Extraction Gating (REQ-SEC-114)**: The `KnowledgeGraph` has a lazy constructor that performs no filesystem creation until a write is explicitly requested. In `--read-only` mode, all mutations are blocked. Implicit standing instruction creation via automatic prompt mining (`autoExtract`) is disabled by default per DEC-15. It can only be enabled explicitly via `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`, which prints a notification whenever a new rule is recorded into `<workspace>/.nexus/knowledge.json`.\n- **MCP Server Environment**: MCP servers start with basic process, locale, proxy (`HTTPS_PROXY`, `NO_PROXY`, …) and CA-trust (`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, …) variables only — not Nexus's own credentials. Give a server the token it needs in its `env` block in `mcp.json`.\n- **Build Identity**: `nexus --version` and `nexus doctor` show the commit the running build was made from, its branch, and whether the source had uncommitted changes (`1.2.1 (cc177be91d15-dirty, feat/tui-surface)`), recorded at `make build`.\n- **Pseudo-Terminal (PTY) Allocation**: Commands requiring TTY capabilities run inside allocated virtual pseudo-terminals via `portable-pty`.\n- **Multi-Provider Failover**: Specify secondary fallback endpoints via `--fallback <url1,url2>` or `$LOCAL_LLM_FALLBACK_ENDPOINTS`. Automatic failover occurs if the primary LLM server drops connection or runs out of GPU VRAM (HTTP 503).\n- **BPE Token Accounting & Context Gauge**: Real-time token breakdown across system instructions, tools schema, user messages, assistant thinking, and tool output payloads with visual ANSI progress gauge.\n- **Structured Context Compaction**: `/compact` distills discarded messages into structured memory (files touched, commands run, architectural decisions) instead of discarding context blindly.\n- **Dynamic Few-Shot Injection**: Context-aware injection of valid tool exemplars (edits, test repair, batch reads) tailored to the active task, eliminating tool hallucination in local models (Qwen 2.5, DeepSeek Coder).\n- **Destructive Command Convenience Tripwire (REQ-SEC-108)**: Shell-words tokenization in TypeScript and Rust blocks obviously destructive commands (`rm -rf /`, `mkfs`, raw block device writes, fork bombs) without false positives on legitimate project commands (`rm -rf ./build`, `rm -rf /tmp/x`, `grep reboot`, `echo shutdown`, `chown -R me /srv`). This denylist is a **convenience tripwire** to catch obvious mistakes, **not a security boundary**. Known bypasses (such as `find / -delete`, `/bin/rm -rf /`, `rm -r -f /`, Python scripts) are documented non-goals that pass by design; the sandbox (`--sandbox`) and approval posture (`review`) form the security boundary.\n- **Verifier Execution Gating (REQ-SEC-119)**: Automated test runs and verifiers (`run_tests`, `/test`, completion oracle) are classified as `exec` capabilities subject to the configured approval mode. In review mode, the first verifier execution prompts for approval, displaying the exact test command and resolved script body from `package.json`, `Makefile`, or `Cargo.toml`; folder trust alone does not authorize it.\n- **Approval Input Safety & Surface Integrity (REQ-SEC-103, TUI-09, TUI-10)**:\n  - In `InteractiveRepl`: Prompts display `[y/N/d/a]`. Lone Enter safely defaults to No (`N`). A 400ms grace period discards typed-ahead input and pastes. Selecting `a` (approve all) requires explicit typing of `all` to confirm.\nAdd a small, local context inventory and deterministic maturity assessment that reuse `AutonomousAgent` getters, `DurableTaskState`/`SessionManager`, existing token estimates, `KnowledgeGraph`, and the common slash router. The inventory stores metadata and token estimates, not prompt plaintext or content hashes. Existing provider adapters remain the only inference transport boundary; this feature does not call providers.\n\n### Data model and interfaces\n\n- `ContextInventoryItem`: stable source-identity id (source type + location + scope; no content hash is persisted), source kind/location, estimated tokens, observed time, freshness (`current`, `stale`, `unknown`), trust state, retention, inclusion reason, retrieval method, and optional source references.\n- `ContextInventorySnapshot`: schema version, capture time, session/task/workspace identity, item list, per-source timing/status/counts, estimate provenance, omissions, completeness, and reason for incomplete or unknown coverage.\n- The persisted snapshot is optional. Older session records remain valid; inventory reconstruction labels transcript freshness unknown and marks session-save coverage partial because the exact provider request is unavailable.\n- `/context inspect [--json] [--record]` measures current client-visible sources on demand and optionally stores a content-free session snapshot; `/context maturity [--json]` emits a deterministic four-dimension assessment with evidence, gaps, limitations, and next improvements.\n\n### Compatibility, security, privacy, and retention\n\n- Existing message and session schemas remain readable. New metadata is additive and source-only; full message and tool content remains in the existing session/archive path.\n- Hashes and locators are diagnostic provenance, not proof that a tool observation is true. Model output is labeled model-generated; tool output is labeled unverified unless an existing verifier receipt binds it.\n- No secret-bearing source text is copied into the inventory or maturity report. Paths are workspace-relative where possible. Inventory snapshots follow the containing session's existing retention/deletion behavior; no cross-user cache is introduced.\n- Workspace changes do not silently make an old observation current. If Nexus has no compatible source fingerprint, freshness remains `unknown`.\n- Inventory collection is local and read-only. Failed or unsupported sources are reported with explicit partial status.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The same session state yields the same item identities, estimates, assessment levels, and evidence ordering (capture timestamps and duration metrics are the only variable fields).\n2. Inventory content contains no source plaintext and distinguishes operator, model, tool, and persisted-knowledge trust states. A non-unknown freshness claim requires an explicit invalidation rule; persistence or resume without revalidation demotes `current` to `unknown`.\n3. Compaction and resume retain predecessor source references or mark reconstructed provenance and freshness as unknown; exports carry the inventory and archive references.\n4. Synchronous source errors and item caps are visible as incomplete with counts; no partial inventory is called complete. This version has no source deadline and makes no timeout guarantee.\n5. Context inspection and maturity assessment are available through the shared REPL/TUI slash router and JSON output is parseable.\n6. A schema-less/legacy session remains loadable and receives conservative reconstructed inventory metadata.\n7. No provider changes, provider-specific headers, or new service dependencies are added.\n\n### Boundary request and deferred work\n\n- A bounded `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` boundary request was recorded in the shared claims log for exact per-inference capture at request construction/admission. The file is actively owned by the GV-1 policy-enforcement slice, so it was not edited. Until its owner releases or applies the integration, `/context inspect` is an on-demand approximation and session snapshots remain explicitly partial.\n- Automatic durable-memory promotion, retrieval ranking changes, and context eviction remain deferred. Existing validation research (`CONTEXT-ENGINEERING-VALIDATION-PLAN.md`) says to validate bounded ingestion and evidence reuse before adopting more complex retrieval policies.\n- No Redis, MCP server, vector database, new provider extension, or autonomous memory learner is justified by measured requirements.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ retrieval on `nexus-cli` located the existing context admission/token-accounting path in `cli/src/agent.ts` (`requestMessages`, `admitContext`) and the durable note provenance logic in the same file, plus the project knowledge graph in `cli/src/knowledge.ts`. The implementation reuses those boundaries and adds a local diagnostic; it rejects adding a second planner, a provider-specific context protocol, or an external retrieval service. XERJ's index reflects committed `origin/main`, so current worktree details were checked directly in source. No reference-project code was copied.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nImplementation worktree: `codex/context-maturity-v130`, based on `release/v1.3.0` commit `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The feature branch remains uncommitted and based on that same commit.\n\n| Check | Result | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `npm --prefix cli run build` | Passed | TypeScript type-check plus the repository's CLI data/build-info generation steps. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` (from `cli/`, isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`) | 8 passed, 0 failed on current run | Provenance, scope-specific IDs, source failure, deterministic caps, bounded merge, separate/insufficient maturity evidence, common route and read-only policy, no provider call/plaintext persistence, saved-session and archive export. One earlier assertion failed because it matched descriptive metadata; it was corrected to check a unique plaintext sentinel and the rerun passed. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_durable_fields.test.ts` | 4 passed, 0 failed | Session round-trip and REPL/sidecar/ApplicationCore durable-state integration. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_export_escaping.test.ts` | 5 passed, 0 failed | Existing export escaping regressions. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Local mock wire fixtures across built-in and operator-defined provider adapters; no live inference. |\n| `git diff --check` | Passed | Whitespace/error check on the implementation worktree. |\n\nAll TypeScript test commands used Node 24.11.1, one file at a time, with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. The full repository suite was not run. No inference provider was modified or contacted by this feature.\n\n### Remaining acceptance gaps\n\n- Exact request-time context capture is blocked by the active `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` ownership claim. A bounded handoff request was recorded in the shared claims log; the current owner is not registered in Agent Mail, so delivery through that channel was unavailable. The diagnostic and persisted snapshots explicitly remain partial until the owner releases the file or applies the small integration.\n- No source deadline exists; synchronous source failures and partial outcomes are visible, but this implementation does not guarantee timeout handling.\n- Freshness has no repository/worktree fingerprint or systematic invalidation, and no retrieval-latency SLO or cache instrumentation exists.\n- The existing operator `/knowledge set` is an explicit write path, but `manage_knowledge` also lets model-generated tool arguments call `KnowledgeGraph.set`; legacy `autoExtract` can write prompt-derived entries when `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`. This context slice did not change those paths because `core/spine_handlers.ts`, `index.ts`, and `agent.ts` are owned by the active GV-1 slice. A boundary request asks that owner to require operator confirmation and preserve provenance/validation metadata. Until that is done, do not enable automatic extraction, and treat every persisted entry as unverified memory rather than evidence. Deletion/expiry enforcement, live provider calls, and held-out task-quality evaluation were not added.\n- No independent review or full-suite run has been completed; qualification remains pending.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":694834010}},"started_at_ms":1795395736665,"completed_at_ms":1795395737360}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T01:02:17.830Z","ordinal":68735,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a205ec-d8e6-7050-8a6d-25ddebd824d5","call_id":"call_adbcd29242f34edd9f3410f0faf40703","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 3944)\nTotal output lines: 88\n\n- **Command Environment Scrubbing**: `run_command` no longer inherits credentials from the Nexus process, with or without `--sandbox`. Variables set in `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, names that look like credentials (`*_API_KEY`, `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET`, `*_PASSWORD`, `AWS_*`, `GH_TOKEN`, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, ...) and proxy URLs with embedded credentials are removed. To pass a variable through deliberately, list its name in `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (comma-separated, trailing `*` for a prefix; operator environment or `config.env` only, never a project `.env`). Commands that relied on `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar must now opt in. **Limit:** without `--sandbox` the command runs as your user: it can still read your files (`~/.config/nexus/config.env`, `~/.ssh`) and its parent processes' environment through `/proc/<pid>/environ`. Use `--sandbox` to hide host processes and `$HOME`.\n- **Worktree merge hardening**: the legacy task-worktree merge (`WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree`, used for `--worktree` task worktrees) refuses a worktree whose `.git` was replaced, and runs Git pinned to that worktree with hooks and `core.fsmonitor` disabled, so a planted hook or fsmonitor in an agent-writable worktree no longer runs code outside it. The repository's own hooks no longer fire on that commit and merge.\n- **In-Process Plugin Execution & Middleware Auditing**: Custom plugin handlers and middleware execute in-process with full user authority. Plugins are loaded only from documented directories: `~/.config/nexus/plugins/`, `~/.nexus/plugins/`, and trusted workspace directories (`.nexus/plugins/`, `plugins/`). The undocumented foreign directory `~/.config/opencode/plugins` has been removed (REQ-SEC-115), and the legacy worker-thread sandbox claim has been retired (`executeSandboxed` deleted, REQ-SEC-117). Any middleware rewriting of tool I/O (`preToolExecution` or `postToolExecution`) is cryptographically audited with before-and-after SHA-256 digests in both `~/.nexus/audit.log` (`kind: \"middleware_rewrite\"`) and the flight log (EXT-004, REQ-SEC-116).\n- **Workspace Trust & Context Provenance (REQ-SEC-113, CTX-001)**: An opened workspace is data until explicitly trusted by the operator. Workspace trust covers:\n  - Project instructions (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md`, `.nexusrules`). In a trusted workspace `@path` imports inside them are expanded (workspace-confined by real path, read once, 5 levels deep at most, regular files up to 64 KiB); the instruction heading lists the loaded files. An untrusted workspace gets neither the file nor its imports.\n  - Workspace `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` server definitions.\n  - Workspace plugins (`.nexus/plugins/`, `plugins/`).\n  - Workspace skills (`skills/`, `.nexus/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`).\n  - Workspace knowledge graph (`.nexus/knowledge.json`).\n  Trust is managed via `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; stored in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). In an untrusted workspace:\n  - Project instructions are not injected into the agent prompt.\n  - Workspace MCP servers and plugins are not loaded.\n  - Workspace skills are not loaded into context; `/skills` lists them as \"untrusted (not loaded)\". Attempting to use `/skill <name>` or the `activate_skill` tool on an untrusted skill is refused with: `Skill '<name>' belongs to an untrusted workspace. Run 'nexus trust .' to trust this workspace.`.\n  - Workspace knowledge is ignored and not loaded into context.\n  - Injected prompt blocks from skills and knowledge are explicitly annotated with source path and trust state (`[source: <path>] [trust: <state>]`).\n  - In `--read-only` mode, untrusted components are never loaded, no writes occur, and `.nexus/` is never created.\n  Servers, plugins, and skills in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always trusted and loaded.\n- **Knowledge Graph Hygiene & Auto-Extraction Gating (REQ-SE…1944 tokens truncated…ctor database, new provider extension, or autonomous memory learner is justified by measured requirements.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ retrieval on `nexus-cli` located the existing context admission/token-accounting path in `cli/src/agent.ts` (`requestMessages`, `admitContext`) and the durable note provenance logic in the same file, plus the project knowledge graph in `cli/src/knowledge.ts`. The implementation reuses those boundaries and adds a local diagnostic; it rejects adding a second planner, a provider-specific context protocol, or an external retrieval service. XERJ's index reflects committed `origin/main`, so current worktree details were checked directly in source. No reference-project code was copied.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nImplementation worktree: `codex/context-maturity-v130`, based on `release/v1.3.0` commit `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The feature branch remains uncommitted and based on that same commit.\n\n| Check | Result | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `npm --prefix cli run build` | Passed | TypeScript type-check plus the repository's CLI data/build-info generation steps. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` (from `cli/`, isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`) | 8 passed, 0 failed on current run | Provenance, scope-specific IDs, source failure, deterministic caps, bounded merge, separate/insufficient maturity evidence, common route and read-only policy, no provider call/plaintext persistence, saved-session and archive export. One earlier assertion failed because it matched descriptive metadata; it was corrected to check a unique plaintext sentinel and the rerun passed. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_durable_fields.test.ts` | 4 passed, 0 failed | Session round-trip and REPL/sidecar/ApplicationCore durable-state integration. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_export_escaping.test.ts` | 5 passed, 0 failed | Existing export escaping regressions. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Local mock wire fixtures across built-in and operator-defined provider adapters; no live inference. |\n| `git diff --check` | Passed | Whitespace/error check on the implementation worktree. |\n\nAll TypeScript test commands used Node 24.11.1, one file at a time, with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. The full repository suite was not run. No inference provider was modified or contacted by this feature.\n\n### Remaining acceptance gaps\n\n- Exact request-time context capture is blocked by the active `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` ownership claim. A bounded handoff request was recorded in the shared claims log; the current owner is not registered in Agent Mail, so delivery through that channel was unavailable. The diagnostic and persisted snapshots explicitly remain partial until the owner releases the file or applies the small integration.\n- No source deadline exists; synchronous source failures and partial outcomes are visible, but this implementation does not guarantee timeout handling.\n- Freshness has no repository/worktree fingerprint or systematic invalidation, and no retrieval-latency SLO or cache instrumentation exists.\n- The existing operator `/knowledge set` is an explicit write path, but `manage_knowledge` also lets model-generated tool arguments call `KnowledgeGraph.set`; legacy `autoExtract` can write prompt-derived entries when `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`. This context slice did not change those paths because `core/spine_handlers.ts`, `index.ts`, and `agent.ts` are owned by the active GV-1 slice. A boundary request asks that owner to require operator confirmation and preserve provenance/validation metadata. Until that is done, do not enable automatic extraction, and treat every persisted entry as unverified memory rather than evidence. Deletion/expiry enforcement, live provider calls, and held-out task-quality evaluation were not added.\n- No independent review or full-suite run has been completed; qualification remains pending.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","create_time":1795395737.8306522}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":30409,"sampled_through_sequence":30406,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
