 or new service dependencies are added.\n\n### Boundary request and deferred work\n\n- A bounded `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` boundary request was recorded in the shared claims log for exact per-inference capture at request construction/admission. The file is actively owned by the GV-1 policy-enforcement slice, so it was not edited. Until its owner releases or applies the integration, `/context inspect` is an on-demand approximation and session snapshots remain explicitly partial.\n- Automatic durable-memory promotion, retrieval ranking changes, and context eviction remain deferred. Existing validation research (`CONTEXT-ENGINEERING-VALIDATION-PLAN.md`) says to validate bounded ingestion and evidence reuse before adopting more complex retrieval policies.\n- No Redis, MCP server, vector database, new provider extension, or autonomous memory learner is justified by measured requirements.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ retrieval on `nexus-cli` located the existing context admission/token-accounting path in `cli/src/agent.ts` (`requestMessages`, `admitContext`) and the durable note provenance logic in the same file, plus the project knowledge graph in `cli/src/knowledge.ts`. The implementation reuses those boundaries and adds a local diagnostic; it rejects adding a second planner, a provider-specific context protocol, or an external retrieval service. XERJ's index reflects committed `origin/main`, so current worktree details were checked directly in source. No reference-project code was copied.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nImplementation worktree: `codex/context-maturity-v130`, based on `release/v1.3.0` commit `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The feature branch remains uncommitted and based on that same commit.\n\n| Check | Result | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `npm --prefix cli run build` | Passed | TypeScript type-check plus the repository's CLI data/build-info generation steps. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` (from `cli/`, isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`) | 8 passed, 0 failed on current run | Provenance, scope-specific IDs, source failure, deterministic caps, bounded merge, separate/insufficient maturity evidence, common route and read-only policy, no provider call/plaintext persistence, saved-session and archive export. One earlier assertion failed because it matched descriptive metadata; it was corrected to check a unique plaintext sentinel and the rerun passed. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_durable_fields.test.ts` | 4 passed, 0 failed | Session round-trip and REPL/sidecar/ApplicationCore durable-state integration. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_export_escaping.test.ts` | 5 passed, 0 failed | Existing export escaping regressions. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Local mock wire fixtures across built-in and operator-defined provider adapters; no live inference. |\n| `git diff --check` | Passed | Whitespace/error check on the implementation worktree. |\n\nAll TypeScript test commands used Node 24.11.1, one file at a time, with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. The full repository suite was not run. No inference provider was modified or contacted by this feature.\n\n### Remaining acceptance gaps\n\n- Exact request-time context capture is blocked by the active `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` ownership claim. A bounded handoff request was recorded in the shared claims log; the current owner is not registered in Agent Mail, so delivery through that channel was unavailable. The diagnostic and persisted snapshots explicitly remain partial until the owner releases the file or applies the small integration.\n- No source deadline exists; synchronous source failures and partial outcomes are visible, but this implementation does not guarantee timeout handling.\n- Freshness has no repository/worktree fingerprint or systematic invalidation, and no retrieval-latency SLO or cache instrumentation exists.\n- The existing operator `/knowledge set` is an explicit write path, but `manage_knowledge` also lets model-generated tool arguments call `KnowledgeGraph.set`; legacy `autoExtract` can write prompt-derived entries when `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`. This context slice did not change those paths because `core/spine_handlers.ts`, `index.ts`, and `agent.ts` are owned by the active GV-1 slice. A boundary request asks that owner to require operator confirmation and preserve provenance/validation metadata. Until that is done, do not enable automatic extraction, and treat every persisted entry as unverified memory rather than evidence. Deletion/expiry enforcement, live provider calls, and held-out task-quality evaluation were not added.\n- No independent review or full-suite run has been completed; qualification remains pending.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":849844412}},"started_at_ms":1795395741112,"completed_at_ms":1795395741963}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T01:02:22.124Z","ordinal":68742,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a205ec-e9ac-7e42-96be-fd62abd359de","call_id":"call_4d7a9bc835ea46eca22c1f84a933138d","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"768-  Servers, plugins, and skills in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always trusted and loaded.\n769:- **Knowledge Graph Hygiene & Auto-Extraction Gating (REQ-SEC-114)**: The `KnowledgeGraph` has a lazy constructor that performs no filesystem creation until a write is explicitly requested. In `--read-only` mode, all mutations are blocked. Implicit standing instruction creation via automatic prompt mining (`autoExtract`) is disabled by default per DEC-15. It can only be enabled explicitly via `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`, which prints a notification whenever a new rule is recorded into `<workspace>/.nexus/knowledge.json`.\n770-- **MCP Server Environment**: MCP servers start with basic process, locale, proxy (`HTTPS_PROXY`, `NO_PROXY`, …) and CA-trust (`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, …) variables only — not Nexus's own credentials. Give a server the token it needs in its `env` block in `mcp.json`.\n771-- **Build Identity**: `nexus --version` and `nexus doctor` show the commit the running build was made from, its branch, and whether the source had uncommitted changes (`1.2.1 (cc177be91d15-dirty, feat/tui-surface)`), recorded at `make build`.\n772-- **Pseudo-Terminal (PTY) Allocation**: Commands requiring TTY capabilities run inside allocated virtual pseudo-terminals via `portable-pty`.\n\n## Design decision record\n\n### Decision\n\nAdd a small, local context inventory and deterministic maturity assessment that reuse `AutonomousAgent` getters, `DurableTaskState`/`SessionManager`, existing token estimates, `KnowledgeGraph`, and the common slash router. The inventory stores metadata and token estimates, not prompt plaintext or content hashes. Existing provider adapters remain the only inference transport boundary; this feature does not call providers.\n\n### Data model and interfaces\n\n- `ContextInventoryItem`: stable source-identity id (source type + location + scope; no content hash is persisted), source kind/location, estimated tokens, observed time, freshness (`current`, `stale`, `unknown`), trust state, retention, inclusion reason, retrieval method, and optional source references.\n- `ContextInventorySnapshot`: schema version, capture time, session/task/workspace identity, item list, per-source timing/status/counts, estimate provenance, omissions, completeness, and reason for incomplete or unknown coverage.\n- The persisted snapshot is optional. Older session records remain valid; inventory reconstruction labels transcript freshness unknown and marks session-save coverage partial because the exact provider request is unavailable.\n- `/context inspect [--json] [--record]` measures current client-visible sources on demand and optionally stores a content-free session snapshot; `/context maturity [--json]` emits a deterministic four-dimension assessment with evidence, gaps, limitations, and next improvements.\n\n### Compatibility, security, privacy, and retention\n\n- Existing message and session schemas remain readable. New metadata is additive and source-only; full message and tool content remains in the existing session/archive path.\n- Hashes and locators are diagnostic provenance, not proof that a tool observation is true. Model output is labeled model-generated; tool output is labeled unverified unless an existing verifier receipt binds it.\n- No secret-bearing source text is copied into the inventory or maturity report. Paths are workspace-relative where possible. Inventory snapshots follow the containing session's existing retention/deletion behavior; no cross-user cache is introduced.\n- Workspace changes do not silently make an old observation current. If Nexus has no compatible source fingerprint, freshness remains `unknown`.\n- Inventory collection is local and read-only. Failed or unsupported sources are reported with explicit partial status.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The same session state yields the same item identities, estimates, assessment levels, and evidence ordering (capture timestamps and duration metrics are the only variable fields).\n2. Inventory content contains no source plaintext and distinguishes operator, model, tool, and persisted-knowledge trust states. A non-unknown freshness claim requires an explicit invalidation rule; persistence or resume without revalidation demotes `current` to `unknown`.\n3. Compaction and resume retain predecessor source references or mark reconstructed provenance and freshness as unknown; exports carry the inventory and archive references.\n4. Synchronous source errors and item caps are visible as incomplete with counts; no partial inventory is called complete. This version has no source deadline and makes no timeout guarantee.\n5. Context inspection and maturity assessment are available through the shared REPL/TUI slash router and JSON output is parseable.\n6. A schema-less/legacy session remains loadable and receives conservative reconstructed inventory metadata.\n7. No provider changes, provider-specific headers, or new service dependencies are added.\n\n### Boundary request and deferred work\n\n- A bounded `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` boundary request was recorded in the shared claims log for exact per-inference capture at request construction/admission. The file is actively owned by the GV-1 policy-enforcement slice, so it was not edited. Until its owner releases or applies the integration, `/context inspect` is an on-demand approximation and session snapshots remain explicitly partial.\n- Automatic durable-memory promotion, retrieval ranking changes, and context eviction remain deferred. Existing validation research (`CONTEXT-ENGINEERING-VALIDATION-PLAN.md`) says to validate bounded ingestion and evidence reuse before adopting more complex retrieval policies.\n- No Redis, MCP server, vector database, new provider extension, or autonomous memory learner is justified by measured requirements.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ retrieval on `nexus-cli` located the existing context admission/token-accounting path in `cli/src/agent.ts` (`requestMessages`, `admitContext`) and the durable note provenance logic in the same file, plus the project knowledge graph in `cli/src/knowledge.ts`. The implementation reuses those boundaries and adds a local diagnostic; it rejects adding a second planner, a provider-specific context protocol, or an external retrieval service. XERJ's index reflects committed `origin/main`, so current worktree details were checked directly in source. No reference-project code was copied.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nImplementation worktree: `codex/context-maturity-v130`, based on `release/v1.3.0` commit `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The feature branch remains uncommitted and based on that same commit.\n\n| Check | Result | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `npm --prefix cli run build` | Passed | TypeScript type-check plus the repository's CLI data/build-info generation steps. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` (from `cli/`, isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`) | 8 passed, 0 failed on current run | Provenance, scope-specific IDs, source failure, deterministic caps, bounded merge, separate/insufficient maturity evidence, common route and read-only policy, no provider call/plaintext persistence, saved-session and archive export. One earlier assertion failed because it matched descriptive metadata; it was corrected to check a unique plaintext sentinel and the rerun passed. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_durable_fields.test.ts` | 4 passed, 0 failed | Session round-trip and REPL/sidecar/ApplicationCore durable-state integration. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_export_escaping.test.ts` | 5 passed, 0 failed | Existing export escaping regressions. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Local mock wire fixtures across built-in and operator-defined provider adapters; no live inference. |\n| `git diff --check` | Passed | Whitespace/error check on the implementation worktree. |\n\nAll TypeScript test commands used Node 24.11.1, one file at a time, with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. The full repository suite was not run. No inference provider was modified or contacted by this feature.\n\n### Remaining acceptance gaps\n\n- Exact request-time context capture is blocked by the active `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts` ownership claim. A bounded handoff request was recorded in the shared claims log; the current owner is not registered in Agent Mail, so delivery through that channel was unavailable. The diagnostic and persisted snapshots explicitly remain partial until the owner releases the file or applies the small integration.\n- No source deadline exists; synchronous source failures and partial outcomes are visible, but this implementation does not guarantee timeout handling.\n- Freshness has no repository/worktree fingerprint or systematic invalidation, and no retrieval-latency SLO or cache instrumentation exists.\n- The existing operator `/knowledge set` is an explicit write path, but `manage_knowledge` also lets model-generated tool arguments call `KnowledgeGraph.set`; legacy `autoExtract` can write prompt-derived entries when `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`. This context slice did not change those paths because `core/spine_handlers.ts`, `index.ts`, and `agent.ts` are owned by the active GV-1 slice. A boundary request asks that owner to require operator confirmation and preserve provenance/validation metadata. Until that is done, do not enable automatic extraction, and treat every persisted entry as unverified memory rather than evidence. Deletion/expiry enforcement, live provider calls, and held-out task-quality evaluation were not added.\n- No independent review or full-suite run has been completed; qualification remains pending.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a205e5-07d1-72b3-b0a4-db59d82d43bb","create_time":1795395742.1246076}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":30412,"sampled_through_sequence":30409,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
