{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:06:13.393Z","ordinal":71040,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20602-56e4-7401-a61d-85bcc9f8019b","session_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","root_turn_id":"01a20602-56e4-7401-a61d-85bcc9f8019b","response_id":"resp_0f4fd85789ec756b016b039f9161c087d29cdddb240220cf57","usage":{"input_tokens":204318,"cached_input_tokens":203648,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":138,"reasoning_output_tokens":61,"total_tokens":204456},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":28953046,"cached_input_tokens":28641536,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":86892,"reasoning_output_tokens":47672,"total_tokens":29039938},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1500163759,"cached_input_tokens":1487549952,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2689893,"reasoning_output_tokens":1347609,"total_tokens":1502853652}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:06:14.420Z","ordinal":71041,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20602-56e4-7401-a61d-85bcc9f8019b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-18d66788-3d2b-4e32-95d1-5afe99de25c8","process_id":"17345","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '86,110p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '86,110p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md","name":"PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"| HK-1 | Declarative command hooks: events `pre-tool`, `post-tool`, `pre-verify`, `post-verify`, `permission-request`, `session-start`, `session-end`, `stop`, `pre-compact`; JSON on stdin (`session_id`, `cwd`, `hook_event_name`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`) and on stdout (`decision`, `reason`, `hookSpecificOutput`), exit 2 blocks with stderr as the reason, a timeout, a scrubbed environment (reuse `buildMcpEnvironment`), results written to the receipt. A hook may only deny or tighten arguments, never grant (Gemini CLI also ignores `allow` from lower tiers). Project-defined hooks need a trust decision keyed by a hash of the command (reference: Gemini CLI `trusted_hooks.json`, goose `hooks/mod.rs`, Codex hook trust). Handler types: command only (no peer shipped http, prompt or subagent handlers in what was read) | a hook that returns `allow` for a denied action has no effect; a changed project hook asks again; the contract is documented so existing hook scripts of that shape transfer | M | P1 |\n| HK-2 | Plugin manifest `nexus-plugin.json` (name, version, declared contributions: hooks, skills, verifiers, MCP servers; required permissions); install from a local path or a git URL **pinned to a commit hash**; capabilities shown at install, resolved commit recorded in an install receipt; `nexus plugins install\\|list\\|remove\\|verify`; no marketplace and no auto-update (reference: Gemini CLI `gemini-extension.json`, goose `.goose-plugin-install.json`). Skills load on a declared trigger and are not always-on (references: OpenHands trigger-based skills, the on-demand-loading guidance of `agent-rules-books`). Also fix the documented example in `docs/SKILLS_AND_PLUGINS.md` that does not match the code (`onTurnStart` positional arguments versus a context object) and stop swallowing plugin load errors silently | installing a plugin whose declared permissions exceed the system policy is refused; `verify` detects a modified install; a skill whose trigger did not fire adds nothing to the request (request-shape snapshot) | M | P1 |\n| HK-3 | Per-plugin trust instead of per-directory, and run workspace-defined plugin hooks as subprocesses rather than in-process (a trusted workspace today runs every `.js` file in it with full process authority) | decision D18; migration note for existing plugins | M | P2 |\n\n### PA: parallel agents and goals\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\n| PA-2 | Verified merge arbitration: when parallel units integrate, apply each diff, run verification on the merged tree, reject on failure. No peer read does more than isolate; this is where Nexus can differ | two units whose changes conflict semantically (each passes alone) fail verification on the merged tree and neither is integrated silently | M | P1 |\n| PA-3 | Goal semantics: a ledger entry active from an earlier process resumes **paused** (opencode `goal.ts` boot id), stall counting by failure fingerprint and not turn count, completion decided only by a verifier receipt, an optional token budget rolling up from sub-operations (reference: Codex goal budget); `/team` leaves experimental only after PA-1 and PA-2 | stale goal resumes paused with a visible reason; a model \"I am done\" does not complete a goal | M | P2 |\n| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\n\n## 5. Sequencing\n\nPhase A (foundation, parallelisable): PF-1, PF-2, GV-4, VR-1, GV-3. These unblock the rest and cost little.\nPhase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2.\nPhase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5.\nPhase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\nPA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\n\nDependencies on earlier plans: v1.2.1 RD-3 (`stream-json` output) feeds PF-2; RD-4 (GitHub Action) pairs with VR-1; CS-9 (per-command approval rules, v1.2.2) and GV-1 must agree on rule semantics (allow < prompt < forbidden, deciding rule recorded); MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split) makes AC-1 and BG-1 much easier and should land first if possible; PR-* (provider registry) is independent; MQ-* (measurement) should publish at least one result before this release claims anything.\n\n## 6. Release gates\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":806846128}},"started_at_ms":1795399573613,"completed_at_ms":1795399574420}}
